    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d81">

                <pb facs="0170" n="158" sameAs="frus:frus1940v01/pg158"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1039/2249: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Sweden</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Sterling</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stockholm</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-04-16T13:00:00+01:00">April 16, 1940—1
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received April 16—10:10 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>282. Following telephoned by Mrs. Harriman at noon.</p>
                <p>All members of the Norwegian Foreign Office have left for Stockholm. Marshal of
                    the Court has arrived at Salen where the Crown Princess with her children are
                    staying. He states that all the members of the Royal Family are well and may go
                    to Stockholm.</p>
                <p>She now knows whereabouts of Norwegian Government across Swedish border. The
                    roads are open and when Losey<anchor n="22" xml:id="d81-f1"/> returns to her at
                    Salen she will proceed with him to Government.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sterling</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d81-f1" n="22" xml:id="d81-f1-content">
                <p>Capt. Robert M. Losey, Assistant Military Attaché in Norway and Sweden.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
